id19 and limit community transmission  ensuring you're vaccinated with all of  your eligible doses  can help protect you against severe  outcomes from the disease  it can also reduce the risk of  experiencing long covid after an  infection  as we go about our daily activities we  must remain mindful  we can each take simple steps to protect  our families ourselves and our  communities  everything we've learned to do since  covid came into our lives such as  masking in places like acute care and  continuing care settings and staying  home and away from others when sick  will help protect those who are most at  risk  and limit any possible spread  thank you and we're happy to take  questions  thanks dr hinshaw no one here in person  so we'll go to the phone we'll take the  first caller please  thank you the first question is from  kevin mimick ctv  dr hanshaw i'm hoping you can provide an  update on the acute juvenile hepatitis  cases in this province  sure we  now have three cases that meet the  probable world health organization  definition which is to say that other  common causes of hepatitis have been  ruled out  when  the update provided by the federal  government was released last week that  third case was added in the reports that  came to us one of those cases was  had been discharged from hospital and  two were still in hospital but at this  time there are no cases in alberta that  have required significant attention such  as a liver transplant  thanks dr hanchov kevin a follow-up  yeah i'm wondering if these are being  traced at all  locally in alberta i understand that  most of the information comes from the  federal government in terms of  classifying the three uh youth as uh  having a probable case of this but  what's being done locally to  determine emerging outbreaks of this  disease  the national reporting comes from us so  at the provincial level we at the  ministry work with our partners in  alberta health services and through them  with frontline p